        <div id="protein-per-100-calories" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Why Protein per 100 Calories Is the Right Measure
          </h2>

          <p>
            Calories per scoop is the number most people look at, and it is the wrong one. A scoop is whatever size the brand decides. A 15 g scoop of a mediocre blend can list 60 calories and look leaner than a 30 g scoop of isolate at 110, while delivering only 9 g of protein. Dividing protein by calories and multiplying by 100 gives you a single number you can compare across any product, flavor or serving size.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a hard ceiling on that number. Food labels count protein at 4 calories per gram, so pure protein works out to 25 g per 100 calories. No powder can genuinely beat that; the only way to get close is to remove nearly all the fat and carbohydrate, which is what whey isolate does. Isopure Zero Carb's label sits right on the ceiling. Every other powder falls short in proportion to how much fat, sugar, fiber or flavoring it carries.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When a label beats the ceiling</h3>

          <p>
            You will occasionally see a label that appears to break 25 g per 100 calories, such as 26 g of protein for 100 calories. That comes from rounding, not magic. FDA rules have labels round calories above 50 to the nearest 10 and protein to the nearest gram, so a scoop with 104 real calories can show 100, and one with 25.6 g of protein can show 26. The difference between two products at 24 and 25 g per 100 calories is within that noise, so do not overthink it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Labels also carry an allowed margin of error. Under FDA compliance rules, the real calorie content can be up to 20 percent above the declared figure before a product is considered misbranded, and protein must be at least 80 percent of the declared amount. Reputable brands land much closer than that, which is one reason we favor certified products, but it is a reason to treat label figures as good estimates rather than precise measurements. Our <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein do I need</a> guide helps you set a target that has room for that error.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lowest-calorie-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which Type of Protein Powder Is Lowest in Calories?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Whey isolate is the lowest-calorie mainstream protein, typically 90 percent protein or more by weight, with almost no fat or lactose. Whey concentrate is usually 70 to 80 percent protein and brings a gram or two more fat and carbohydrate per scoop, which lowers it to about 18 to 20 g of protein per 100 calories. Casein sits around the same level as concentrate. Egg white powder is close to isolate on calories, though it is high in sodium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plant isolates can match whey on this measure. Pea protein isolate is around 80 to 85 percent protein, and Naked Pea's label of about 27 g in 120 calories puts it level with flavored whey isolates. Soy isolate is similar. Blends are where plant powders lose ground: added fiber, seed proteins, gums, flavors and sweeteners push most flavored plant blends down to 14 to 18 g per 100 calories. Hemp, almond and pumpkin seed proteins are lower still, because they keep fat and fiber from the seed.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">A note on collagen</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">High on the label, low on usefulness</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Collagen peptides list about 18 g of protein for roughly 70 calories, which looks like the best ratio of all. But collagen lacks tryptophan and is low in leucine, so it does not support muscle the way whey or pea does. Count it as a top-up, not as your low-calorie main protein.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Clear whey drinks deserve a mention because they are often marketed as light. Most are whey isolate with flavor, so they land around 20 g of protein for 80 to 100 calories, which is excellent. Meal replacement shakes and mass gainers sit at the other end: they are designed to supply calories, so their protein per 100 calories is low by design. That is fine for their purpose but wrong for this one. Our <a href="/best-clear-protein-powder/">best clear protein powder</a> guide compares clear isolates in detail.
          </p>
