          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the lowest calorie protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Isopure Zero Carb Unflavored lists about 25 g of protein for 100 calories per scoop, the practical ceiling, since protein counts as 4 calories per gram. Klean Isolate lists about 20 g for 85 calories. Among flavored powders, Isopure Infusions lists about 20 g for 90 calories, per labels at the time of writing.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Which protein powder has the most protein per calorie?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Unflavored whey isolates lead, at roughly 22 to 25 g of protein per 100 calories. Isopure Zero Carb Unflavored is about 25 g, Klean Isolate about 23.5 g and NOW Sports Whey Isolate about 22 g. Naked Pea, at about 22.5 g, is the best plant option we found.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is whey isolate lower in calories than whey concentrate?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes, slightly. Isolate is typically 90 percent protein or more, while concentrate is about 70 to 80 percent, with a little more fat and lactose. That usually means 10 to 30 more calories per 25 g of protein for concentrate, or about 18 to 20 g of protein per 100 calories versus 22 to 25 g for isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is low calorie protein powder good for weight loss?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can help. Protein is filling, costs more energy to digest than carbohydrate or fat, and helps preserve muscle in a calorie deficit. A lean shake makes a high protein target easier to hit within your calories. It only helps if it replaces something or fits your plan; adding it on top adds calories.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How many calories should a protein shake have?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For a snack, a shake made with an isolate and water or unsweetened almond milk lands around 100 to 140 calories. With skim milk, about 190 to 210. As a meal replacement, 300 to 400 calories with fruit, milk and a fat source is reasonable. The right number depends on your goals and the rest of your day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is plant protein lower in calories than whey?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not usually. Pea protein isolate can match whey, and Naked Pea lists about 27 g of protein for 120 calories. Most flavored plant blends are higher in calories per gram of protein, around 14 to 18 g per 100 calories, because of added fiber, fats, flavors and seed proteins.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are there protein powders under 100 calories?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Yes. Klean Isolate lists about 85 calories for 20 g of protein, Isopure Infusions about 90 for 20 g and Isopure Zero Carb about 100 for 25 g, per labels at the time of writing. Smaller scoops of other powders can also come in under 100, but check that protein per 100 calories stays above about 20 g.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

